add('A zeta spiral is a sampling layout with a conjugation symmetry','2026-04-14T20:38:43Z','U','Analytic number theory and sampling',
'Which mathematical properties survive rearranging critical-line zeta samples into an integer spiral?',
'The user’s construction places Z_k=ζ(1/2+ikT) in an Ulam-style spiral. Rearranging samples is a visualization map, not an arithmetic correspondence between spiral coordinates and primes. A precise symmetry and index-preservation statement remain available.',
['Fix real T>0 and define s_k=1/2+ikT for integer k. Let p be any bijection from a finite index set to grid cells, such as a square spiral.','The displayed field is Z(p(k))=ζ(s_k). Modulus and phase are separate observables; phase is undefined at an exact zero unless a convention is supplied.'],
'Conjugation and layout invariance',
'ζ(1/2−ikT)=conj ζ(1/2+ikT). Any bijective layout preserves the multiset of sampled values, but it does not preserve adjacency-based statistics under arbitrary reordering.',
'For Re s>1, conjugate the absolutely convergent Dirichlet series Σn^(−s) term by term, obtaining ζ(conj s)=conjζ(s). Both sides extend meromorphically, so the identity holds away from the pole at one by uniqueness of analytic continuation. The multiset statement follows because a bijection merely permutes indices. For adjacency, a sequence (0,0,1,1) has one signaled jump in its linear ordering, while the permutation (0,1,0,1) has three, even though the values and histogram agree. Thus apparent spatial structures may reflect the chosen embedding and should be tested against index-preserving alternatives or permutation baselines.',
'Compare k=1 and k=−1, and compare two four-cell layouts of the same samples.',
'The pair has equal modulus and opposite phase modulo 2π whenever neither value is zero. A grid permutation leaves their individual values unchanged but can move them from adjacent cells to opposite corners.',
'The sampling construction is precisely defined without treating its visual pattern as a theorem about integer arithmetic.',
'No values or zero locations are fabricated, and no claim is made that a finite spiral detects or proves the Riemann hypothesis.',
'Declare a spatial statistic, sampling step and phase convention, then compare it with randomized layouts and nearby T values using the same ordered sample set.',['gamma'])
add('Path differences lie in a circulation kernel, with a useful converse on a finite DAG','2026-04-28T17:45:36Z','A','Incidence geometry',
'What exactly follows from the input-dependent segmentation incidence matrix?',
'The source correctly warns that a published basis for another matrix cannot substitute for the actual input-dependent B_x. Its path-difference identity is immediate; on a finite directed acyclic graph a stronger spanning statement follows from flow decomposition.',
['Let a finite directed acyclic graph have source s and sink t, with every edge lying on an s-to-t path. Orient its incidence matrix B with −1 at tails. Let χ(p) be the edge-indicator vector of a directed path.','Every such path satisfies Bχ(p)=e_t−e_s. Fix one reference path p₀.'],
'Path-difference spanning theorem',
'B(χ(p)−χ(p₀))=0 for every path. Moreover the real kernel of B is spanned by these path differences under the stated DAG and edge-coverage hypotheses.',
'The kernel identity is subtraction. For the converse, take z∈ker B and construct a strictly positive feasible s-to-t flow f by summing one covering path for each edge. For sufficiently small ε>0, both f and f+εz remain nonnegative and have the same divergence. Every nonnegative flow on a finite DAG decomposes into nonnegative path flows: start at a positive source edge, follow conservation until reaching the sink, subtract the smallest path-edge flow, and repeat; acyclicity prevents an endless loop. Therefore f and f+εz are linear combinations of path indicators with the same total coefficient, equal to their source flow. Subtracting cancels that total and expresses εz as a linear combination of differences from p₀.',
'Use a diamond s→a→t and s→b→t.',
'In edge order (sa,at,sb,bt), the two paths have vectors (1,1,0,0) and (0,0,1,1). Their difference (1,1,−1,−1) spans the one-dimensional incidence kernel.',
'The exact matrix and graph determine both the kernel and its path interpretation.',
'The spanning proof uses a finite DAG and edge coverage. Directed cycles can add circulation components requiring separate cycle generators.',
'For each actual input x, publish B_x, enumerate its admissible path polytope and certify a basis with exact arithmetic before comparing any external reference basis.',['convex'])
add('A Čech-type cocycle is removable exactly when its loop periods vanish','2026-04-16T13:39:16Z','U','Cohomology and descent',
'What does a triadic compatibility condition prove, and when can a finite-period cover trivialize a class?',
'The source uses local-state cochains, triangle conditions and period-k trivialization. A finite graph model makes the obstruction exact. It also shows that integer-valued nonzero periods are not automatically removed by passing to a finite cover.',
['For an oriented graph, let g_ij=−g_ji be an additive edge cochain with values in an abelian group A. A potential f gives the coboundary g_ij=f_j−f_i.','On a filled triangular overlap, the cocycle condition is g_ij+g_jk+g_ki=0. On a graph without filled faces, loop periods must be tested separately.'],
'Loop criterion and coefficient-dependent cover behavior',
'On a connected graph, g is a coboundary if and only if its sum around every closed walk is zero. On S¹ with integer coefficients, a degree-k cover multiplies a cohomology class by k, so no nonzero class is killed by a finite cover.',
'Coboundary sums telescope around a loop. Conversely, fix a base vertex and define f(v) as the sum of g along any path from the base to v. Zero periods make this independent of path, and appending an edge proves g_ij=f_j−f_i. For a circle, the cohomology class is its total period n. A degree-k covering traverses the base loop k times, so the pulled-back period is kn. Over Z this is nonzero whenever k,n are nonzero. Over Z/mZ it can vanish, for example when k=m. Thus period trivialization is a theorem about coefficient torsion and the chosen cover, not a universal consequence of refinement.',
'Use a three-edge loop with edge values (1,0,0).',
'The period is one, so no integer potential removes it. A double cover has period two, still nonzero over Z. With coefficients Z/2Z, that same doubled period is zero and a potential can be constructed on the cover.',
'The local/global distinction and the role of coefficients are explicitly certified.',
'General sheaf cohomology involves restriction maps and a cover or direct-limit construction; the graph proof does not identify every proposed temporal obstruction.',
'Specify the coefficient sheaf and allowed refinements, prove compatibility of pullbacks with coboundaries, and determine the torsion subgroup on which finite-period trivialization can occur.',['convex'])
add('The silver-mean substitution has exact eigenvalues and normalized letter frequencies','2026-04-21T15:09:42Z','A','Substitution dynamics',
'Which frequency is actually determined by the recovered silver-mean substitution matrix?',
'The source writes M=[[2,1],[1,0]], λ=1+√2 and λ⁻¹=√2−1 as a frequency. The reciprocal is a useful slope parameter, but the normalized two-letter frequencies follow from a normalized eigenvector and are different numbers.',
['Let the substitution be a↦aab and b↦a, with columns of M counting letters in the images. Then M=[[2,1],[1,0]].','The substitution is primitive because M² has all positive entries. Let v be its positive right eigenvector, normalized so its entries sum to one.'],
'Exact substitution frequencies',
'The eigenvalues are 1±√2. A positive right eigenvector is (λ,1), so f_a=λ/(λ+1)=1/√2 and f_b=1/(λ+1)=1−1/√2. Their ratio f_b/f_a is λ⁻¹=√2−1.',
'The characteristic polynomial is t²−2t−1. For λ=1+√2, M(λ,1)=(2λ+1,λ)=(λ²,λ)=λ(λ,1). Normalize by λ+1 to obtain frequencies. To justify the limit, decompose the count vector M^ne_a along the two eigenvectors. The second contribution is proportional to (1−√2)^n, whose magnitude relative to λ^n tends to zero because |1−√2|<λ. Dividing by total word length therefore yields the positive normalized eigenvector. The reciprocal λ⁻¹ equals the ratio of frequencies, not either individual frequency.',
'Start from the letter a.',
'The first count vectors are (1,0),(2,1),(5,2),(12,5). The last a-frequency is 12/17≈0.705882, approaching 1/√2≈0.707107; the b-frequency approaches about 0.292893, rather than √2−1≈0.414214.',
'The matrix supports an exact asymptotic frequency theorem with the normalization corrected.',
'A gap-label group for a particular Schrödinger hull needs its own crossed-product and trace theorem. The substitution eigenvalue alone does not prove every spectral label or gap opens.',
'Construct the invariant measure on the hull, compute cylinder frequencies and then identify its ordered trace group under the precise operator-algebra model.',['hps','rieffel'])
add('The roots of x⁴−2 have a dihedral Galois action, not a physical trajectory','2026-04-23T04:25:37Z','A','Galois theory',
'Can the proposed D₄ root orbit be proved with explicit field automorphisms?',
'The recovered example usefully distinguishes symmetry transport from motion. The four roots form a dihedral permutation representation once the splitting field and its degree are established. No force law or time parameter enters the statement.',
['Let a be the positive real fourth root of two. The roots of x⁴−2 are a,ia,−a,−ia, and the splitting field is K=Q(a,i).','Write D₄ for the symmetry group of a square of order eight. Define ρ(a)=ia,ρ(i)=i and let τ be complex conjugation.'],
'Explicit dihedral splitting group',
'[K:Q]=8 and Gal(K/Q)=⟨ρ,τ:ρ⁴=τ²=1,τρτ=ρ⁻¹⟩≅D₄.',
'Eisenstein at two makes x⁴−2 irreducible over Q, so [Q(a):Q]=4. The field Q(a) is real and does not contain i; adjoining i doubles the degree, giving eight. Since [K:Q(i)]=4, the same polynomial is irreducible over Q(i), and replacing a by ia defines a Q(i)-automorphism ρ of order four. Conjugation τ has order two and sends ia to −ia; checking on the generators a and i gives τρτ=ρ⁻¹. The eight maps ρ^j and τρ^j are distinct: the first four fix i while the other four conjugate it, and their images of a distinguish j. A degree-eight splitting field has exactly these eight automorphisms.',
'Order the roots as (a,ia,−a,−ia).',
'ρ acts by the four-cycle (1 2 3 4). τ fixes roots one and three and swaps two and four. Composing τρτ sends a to −ia, matching ρ⁻¹(a).',
'The symmetry orbit is fully certified and its algebraic type is explicit.',
'An automorphism permutes algebraic values while fixing Q; it is not a geometric transport law in time or evidence for a physical oscillator.',
'Compare the root action with the square’s vertex representation, compute fixed subfields of its subgroups and identify which symmetry information survives each quotient.',['convex'])
